1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.103
Welcome to Agentic Knowledge Graph Construction

2
00:00:03.303 --> 00:00:04.904
built in partnership with Neo4J.

3
00:00:05.138 --> 00:00:07.941
In this course, you'll design a multi-agent system

4
00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:11.611
that transforms your structured and unstructured data into a knowledge graph.

5
00:00:12.145 --> 00:00:15.648
I've found knowledge graphs really useful for high stakes applications

6
00:00:15.749 --> 00:00:19.886
where accuracy of the information we store and retrieve is important.

7
00:00:20.286 --> 00:00:21.688
This course will teach you why, and

8
00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:23.289
also give you a great set of tools

9
00:00:23.289 --> 00:00:27.093
to building these knowledge graphs. I'm delighted that the instructor

10
00:00:27.093 --> 00:00:29.462
for this course is Andreas Kollegger,

11
00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:33.600
who is a developer evangelist for generative AI at Neo4J.

12
00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:37.203
Thanks, Andrew. I'm excited to be back here and

13
00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.273
to work with you on this course. Similar to RAG,

14
00:00:40.273 --> 00:00:42.242
a knowledge graph system

15
00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.377
splits your text documents into chunks

16
00:00:44.377 --> 00:00:46.546
and stores them in a vector database.

17
00:00:47.047 --> 00:00:48.681
But in addition to that,

18
00:00:48.681 --> 00:00:51.051
it places the chunks into a graph structure,

19
00:00:51.051 --> 00:00:53.253
where each chunk is linked to entities

20
00:00:53.253 --> 00:00:55.422
extracted from it from the chunks.

21
00:00:55.789 --> 00:00:58.491
For example, a text chunk from a product review

22
00:00:58.491 --> 00:01:01.394
can contain entities such as product orders

23
00:01:01.394 --> 00:01:03.463
or delivery issues or product defects.

24
00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:05.398
To build a knowledge graph, you

25
00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:07.267
extract the relevant entities from the chunks,

26
00:01:07.267 --> 00:01:11.237
then connect the chunks and the entities in a graph through edges.

27
00:01:11.471 --> 00:01:14.140
Here, each edge represents a relationship.

28
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:17.944
For example, maybe it represents that this chunk mentioned a certain product

29
00:01:17.944 --> 00:01:21.548
or that that product has an issue. The entities

30
00:01:21.548 --> 00:01:24.250
are retrieved alongside the relevant chunks

31
00:01:24.651 --> 00:01:27.253
to provide more relevant context to the LLM

32
00:01:27.253 --> 00:01:31.157
to generate more precise and accurate answers.

33
00:01:31.858 --> 00:01:34.294
You can also connect this type of

34
00:01:34.294 --> 00:01:36.763
graph to another graph containing additional information

35
00:01:36.763 --> 00:01:40.467
extracted from structured data such as CSV files.

36
00:01:41.668 --> 00:01:43.470
In this course, Andreas will walk you through

37
00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:45.472
how to build a multi-agent system that

38
00:01:45.472 --> 00:01:46.940
helps you do all this work

39
00:01:46.940 --> 00:01:48.641
of constructing this type of knowledge graph.

40
00:01:48.808 --> 00:01:52.779
To transform your structured and unstructured data into a knowledge graph,

41
00:01:53.313 --> 00:01:55.381
you first need to decide on the graph schema,

42
00:01:55.949 --> 00:01:57.650
meaning what types of entities or

43
00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:59.652
nodes you can extract from the data

44
00:02:00.086 --> 00:02:02.188
and what relationships exist between them.

45
00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.559
Once you have the schema defined, you can construct the actual graph

46
00:02:06.559 --> 00:02:08.228
and store it in a graph database.

47
00:02:08.895 --> 00:02:11.397
Instead of mainly looking at your data to find the graph schema,

48
00:02:11.865 --> 00:02:15.802
you will design a multi-agent system for doing that using Google's ADK

49
00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:20.607
or Agent Development Kit. After you learn the basic syntax with ADK,

50
00:02:20.707 --> 00:02:23.209
you'll design your system one agent at a time.

51
00:02:23.710 --> 00:02:25.879
The first agent will converse with you to extract the

52
00:02:25.879 --> 00:02:28.014
goal and the type of graph you want to build.

53
00:02:28.715 --> 00:02:30.617
Then given that goal,

54
00:02:30.717 --> 00:02:33.219
a group of agents will specialize in extracting

55
00:02:33.219 --> 00:02:35.722
the entities and relationships from your structured data,

56
00:02:35.822 --> 00:02:38.925
and another group of agents will handle your unstructured data.

57
00:02:39.626 --> 00:02:42.996
Finally, the final set of agents will connect the two models and

58
00:02:42.996 --> 00:02:47.133
construct a graph accordingly. Many people have worked to create this course.

59
00:02:47.200 --> 00:02:48.835
I'd like to thank from Neo4J,

60
00:02:49.669 --> 00:02:52.505
Martin O'Hanlon and Adam Cowley. and from DeepLearning.AI,

61
00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:53.907
From DeepLearning.AI,

62
00:02:53.907 --> 00:02:58.044
Christopher Policastro and Hawraa Salami
also contributed to this course.

63
00:02:58.311 --> 00:03:00.146
In the first lesson, you learn more

64
00:03:00.146 --> 00:03:02.248
about the underlying structure of knowledge graphs and

65
00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:05.251
how you can build one and use it to identify root causes

66
00:03:05.251 --> 00:03:09.656
of product issues. Sounds great. Let's get started.
